<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<sdf version='1.9'>
  <model name='x500_mono_cam_down'>
    <self_collide>false</self_collide>
    <include merge='true'>
      <uri>x500</uri>
    </include>


<!-- ===================== DUMMY BLUE (sağ / right side) ===================== -->
<!--
  PLACEMENT FIX: was Y=0 Z=-0.30 vs red's Y=0 Z=-0.35, i.e. stacked directly
  on top of each other on the vertical centerline. Real payload mounting is
  symmetric left/right at the same height, so both links now share Z=-0.32
  and only differ in Y. base_link follows PX4's standard body frame
  (Forward-X, Right-Y, Down-Z), so +Y is the vehicle's RIGHT side and -Y is
  LEFT: blue is at +0.20 (right), red is at -0.20 (left).

  RESTORE NOTE: an earlier pass here mistakenly assumed the compiled
  PayloadDropSystem plugin worked by removing this link's own fixed joint,
  and changed gravity/mass/collision/self_collide accordingly. The actually
  compiled plugin (src/modules/simulation/gz_plugins/payload_drop, verified
  against the built .so's own path) works completely differently: it finds
  this dummy link BY NAME, removes only its <visual>/<collision> on drop,
  and spawns a brand new independent falling model via the world "create"
  service: this link itself stays fixed-jointed to base_link forever, it
  never becomes dynamic. None of gravity/mass/collision/self_collide here
  matter to that mechanism, so this is back to its original simple form.
-->
<!-- ===================== DUMMY RED (sol / left side) ===================== -->
<!--
  ADR-011 (2026-08-17): PayloadDropSystem is replaced by two DetachableJoint
  instances, and the two dummy payload links above are gone.

  Why: PayloadDropSystem released a payload by SPAWNING a brand-new model
  through the world "create" service. Measured on 2026-08-17, a body spawned
  that way gets no reliable collision pairs in this gz-sim 8.15 build: a real
  drop was logged at z=-0.72 and still falling 3 s later, and a
  payload-equivalent probe fell through bare ground at every offset tested
  while an identical probe rested fine on the world-loaded drone. So every
  mission drop was falling through the world instead of landing on a target.

  DetachableJoint keeps real drop physics (the payload is a normal dynamic
  body that falls, bounces and settles) while guaranteeing collision pairs,
  because both bodies exist from world load rather than being created
  mid-run. Release = one message on the detach topic, which mirrors the real
  180-degree servo: left rotation frees the LEFT payload, right the RIGHT.

  Mount side is dictated by the existing, UNCHANGED servo->colour mapping in
  gz_payload_actuator.py: release_payload_at_mavi_altigen() -> "red",
  release_payload_at_kirmizi_ucgen() -> "blue". The dummies' SIDES are
  kept (blue LEFT +y, red RIGHT -y) but not their offsets: those dummies
  hung 0.32 m below base_link, which is underground for a landed vehicle,
  and they got away with it only because they had gravity and collision
  disabled. A real body cannot. The mount points now live in the world SDF
  (0, -+0.28, 0.025), chosen to clear the landing gear; see the F2 note
  there for why 0.20 m did not.
-->
<plugin filename="gz-sim-detachable-joint-system" name="gz::sim::systems::DetachableJoint">
  <parent_link>base_link</parent_link>
  <child_model>payload_red</child_model>
  <child_link>link</child_link>
  <detach_topic>/payload/detach/red</detach_topic>
</plugin>

<plugin filename="gz-sim-detachable-joint-system" name="gz::sim::systems::DetachableJoint">
  <parent_link>base_link</parent_link>
  <child_model>payload_blue</child_model>
  <child_link>link</child_link>
  <detach_topic>/payload/detach/blue</detach_topic>
</plugin>




<!-- ===================== KANCA (HOOK) MEKANIZMASI ===================== -->
<!--
  Operator istegi (2026-08-21): kameranin HIZASINDA (z=+0.05, mono_cam
  "0.35 0 .05" ile ayni yukseklik) ama govdenin DIGER ucunda (x=-0.35) bir
  silindir govde, altindan asagi sarkan bir ip ve ucunda kanca.

  NEDEN BU BLOK VAR: HookAttachSystem
  (src/modules/simulation/gz_plugins/hook_attach) derlenmis durumda ve
  /hook/attach, /hook/detach, /hook/state topic'lerini dinliyor - ama bugune
  kadar hicbir .sdf/.world dosyasi onu ornekleme(instantiate) etmedigi icin
  o topic'lere publish etmek hicbir sey tetiklemiyordu; bkz.
  gz_payload_actuator.py'nin kendi notu (release_payload_at_kirmizi_ucgen
  altinda). Bu blok o boslugu kapatir. Gorev 3'un alma/birakma akisi HALA
  mevcut simule yolunda: burasi yalnizca mekanizmayi SDF'ye koyar.

  YUKSEKLIKLER (hepsi base_link'e gore; model yerdeyken base_link z=0.24):
    inis takimi tupu  z = -0.2195, yaricap 0.0069 -> en alt nokta -0.2264
    kanca ucu         z = -0.16,   yaricap 0.02   -> en alt nokta -0.1800
  Kanca ucu iniş takimindan 4.6 cm YUKARIDA kalir, yani yere once daima
  skid deger. Bu kasitli: dunya SDF'sindeki F2 notu (payload_red uzerinde)
  0.20 m'de payload hacminin skid hacmine girdigini, DART'in eklem
  cozuldugu an ic ice gecmis cifti gorup payload'i FIRLATTIGINI kayda
  geciyor (payload 2 hedeften 4.9 m uzaga gitmisti). Sarkan bir govde ayni
  sinifta, o yuzden aciklik payload'inkiyle ayni titizlikte secildi.
  Egilme paylari: uc x=-0.35'te oldugundan 0.046/0.35 -> ~7.5 derece pitch'e
  kadar skid hala once deger. Daha uzun ip isteniyorsa bu sayi dusecektir.

  KUTLE: winch 0.020 + ip/uc 0.015 = 0.035 kg, 2.07 kg govdede %1.7. Dunya
  SDF'si gercek payload kutlesini 1.05 -> 0.15 kg'a bilerek dusuruyor cunku
  MPC_THR_HOVER ve itki/agirlik orani bu SITL modelinin geldigi ayarin
  disina cikiyor; ayni gerekce burada da gecerli, o yuzden kanca hafif.

  TEMAS: contact sensoru yalnizca hook_tip_collision'a bakar (ipin govdesine
  degil) - "kanca dokundu" sorusunun fiziksel karsiligi ucun degmesidir.
  gz-sim-contact-system server.config'de zaten yuklu.
-->
<!--
  MOUNT POINT - THE ONE PLACE TO MOVE THE HOOK.
  Every hook link below is posed relative to this frame, so changing this
  single <pose> relocates winch + rope + Fusion hook body together.

  X = -0.090 IS NOT FREE. The mission side hard-codes it:
  .scripts/olds/v32/v32_flight_stack/gz_system/gz_payload_actuator.py
      HOOK_TIP_BODY_X_M = -0.090
  which is how _hook_tip_xy() converts vehicle pose into hook-tip world XY,
  and therefore how magnet_gap_m() decides whether a pickup is in range
  (HOOK_MAGNET_CAPTURE_RADIUS_M = 0.05). Move this X without moving that
  constant and every Gorev 3 pickup silently mis-measures by the delta.
-->
<frame name="hook_mount" attached_to="base_link">
  <pose relative_to="base_link">-0.090 0 0.05 0 0 0</pose>
</frame>

<link name="hook_winch_link">
  <pose relative_to="hook_mount">0 0 0 1.5707 0 0</pose>
  <inertial>
    <mass>0.020</mass>
    <inertia>
      <ixx>1.5e-5</ixx><iyy>1.5e-5</iyy><izz>9.0e-6</izz>
      <ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyz>0</iyz>
    </inertia>
  </inertial>
  <visual name="winch_visual">
    <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.03</radius><length>0.08</length></cylinder></geometry>
    <material>
      <ambient>0.25 0.25 0.28 1</ambient>
      <diffuse>0.35 0.35 0.40 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.4 0.4 0.4 1</specular>
    </material>
  </visual>
</link>

<joint name="HookWinchJoint" type="fixed">
  <parent>base_link</parent>
  <child>hook_winch_link</child>
</joint>

<link name="hook_rope_link">
  <pose relative_to="hook_mount">0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
  <inertial>
    <!--
      0.015 -> 0.010 kg. This link used to carry BOTH the rope and the hook
      tip; the tip is now a real body of its own (hook_body_link, 0.005 kg)
      carrying the Fusion geometry. The TOTAL hook budget is unchanged at
      0.020 + 0.010 + 0.005 = 0.035 kg, which is the whole point: the note
      above on this mechanism records that the hook is kept light on purpose
      because MPC_THR_HOVER and the thrust/weight ratio of this SITL model
      are tuned for a 2.07 kg airframe. Inertia scaled by the same 2/3 so the
      link stays self-consistent.
    -->
    <mass>0.001</mass>
    <inertia>
      <ixx>1.0e-7</ixx><iyy>1.0e-7</iyy><izz>1.0e-7</izz>
      <ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyz>0</iyz>
    </inertia>
  </inertial>

  <!-- No visual: this link is the cord's exit point, hidden inside the drum.
       The visible rope is the four hinged segments declared below. -->

  <!-- Kanca ucu artik ayri bir link: bkz. hook_body_link (Fusion CAD). -->
</link>

<!-- ============ FUSION HOOK BODY (Payload Hook V4 "M3 NUT") ============ -->
<!--
  WHAT CHANGED: the hook tip was a 24 mm sphere. It is now the actual
  Fusion assembly - Core_Lower + Core_Upper + Servo_Cam + 4x Locking_Arm -
  exported from Payload_Hook_V4_OnePieceArm_Assembly v7.

  Fusion is the source of truth. Nothing here is hand-modelled. The meshes
  come from ~/KURSAD40/cad_exports/hook/ via
      Tools/simulation/gz/cad/update_hook_from_cad.sh
  and are shared byte-for-byte with the standalone model
      Tools/simulation/gz/models/kursad_hook/model.sdf
  so the two copies cannot drift.

  UNITS: Fusion works in mm, Gazebo in metres. The 1/1000 is BAKED INTO THE
  VERTICES by the converter, not applied with <mesh><scale>, so what is
  measured offline is exactly what Gazebo loads. Verified against three
  numbers the CAD report publishes independently: cam OD 26.400 mm, cam
  minimum vertex radius 3.100 mm, retracted arm envelope 25.50 mm.

  MESH FRAME: z = 0 is the rope attach point (Core_Upper top face). The hook
  hangs DOWN from there:
      hook top     base_link z = -0.1330   (exactly where the sphere sat)
      hook bottom  base_link z = -0.19765  (64.65 mm of CAD below it)
      shell        45 mm dia, locked arms 36 mm dia -> inside the shell

  GROUND/PAYLOAD CLEARANCE (base_link is at z = 0.240 when landed):
      landing skid underside   -0.2264  -> hook clears it by 29.4 mm
      payload_red/blue bottom  -0.2400  -> hook tip is 42.4 mm above ground
  The skid still touches down before the hook up to ~18 deg of pitch at this
  X, which is the same class of check the winch note above makes.

  ARM STATE: shown LOCKED (nose radius 18.00 mm = the frozen 36.00 mm locked
  envelope), because the mission starts with the payload attached. The
  RETRACTED mesh is generated alongside it; swapping the four URIs to
  locking_arm_retracted.stl shows the released state. Arm motion is NOT
  simulated - see the collision note below.
-->
<link name="hook_body_link">
  <pose relative_to="hook_rope_seg_4">0 0 -0.02287 0 0 0</pose>
  <inertial>
    <pose>0 0 -0.032 0 0 0</pose>
    <!--
      0.005 -> 0.020 kg. The hook budget is now 0.051 kg
      (0.020 winch + 0.001 anchor + 4 x 0.0025 cord + 0.020 hook), up from
      0.035. That ceiling was set when this was a decorative sphere; the real
      printed V4 hook plus its MG90S is ~55 g, so 20 g is still an
      UNDER-estimate, and the extra 16 g is 0.8% of the 2.07 kg airframe
      against the +14.5% each 0.15 kg payload already adds. Hover tuning does
      not notice it; the solver does - see the cord note above for the crash
      this conditioning is fixing.
    -->
    <mass>0.020</mass>
    <inertia>
      <ixx>9.6e-6</ixx><iyy>9.6e-6</iyy><izz>5.3e-6</izz>
      <ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyz>0</iyz>
    </inertia>
  </inertial>

  <visual name="hook_core_lower_visual">
    <geometry><mesh><uri>model://kursad_hook/meshes/core_lower.stl</uri></mesh></geometry>
    <material>
      <ambient>0.18 0.18 0.20 1</ambient>
      <diffuse>0.28 0.28 0.32 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.30 0.30 0.30 1</specular>
    </material>
  </visual>
  <visual name="hook_core_upper_visual">
    <geometry><mesh><uri>model://kursad_hook/meshes/core_upper.stl</uri></mesh></geometry>
    <material>
      <ambient>0.18 0.18 0.20 1</ambient>
      <diffuse>0.28 0.28 0.32 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.30 0.30 0.30 1</specular>
    </material>
  </visual>
  <visual name="hook_servo_cam_visual">
    <geometry><mesh><uri>model://kursad_hook/meshes/servo_cam.stl</uri></mesh></geometry>
    <material>
      <ambient>0.10 0.20 0.30 1</ambient>
      <diffuse>0.15 0.35 0.55 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.40 0.40 0.40 1</specular>
    </material>
  </visual>

  <!-- One mesh, four instances. The arm is modelled on the +X radius, so
       yaw alone places all four; no duplicated geometry in memory. -->
  <visual name="hook_locking_arm_0_visual">
    <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
    <geometry><mesh><uri>model://kursad_hook/meshes/locking_arm_locked.stl</uri></mesh></geometry>
    <material>
      <ambient>0.55 0.45 0.05 1</ambient>
      <diffuse>0.85 0.70 0.10 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.5 0.5 0.5 1</specular>
    </material>
  </visual>
  <visual name="hook_locking_arm_1_visual">
    <pose>0 0 0 0 0 1.5707963</pose>
    <geometry><mesh><uri>model://kursad_hook/meshes/locking_arm_locked.stl</uri></mesh></geometry>
    <material>
      <ambient>0.55 0.45 0.05 1</ambient>
      <diffuse>0.85 0.70 0.10 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.5 0.5 0.5 1</specular>
    </material>
  </visual>
  <visual name="hook_locking_arm_2_visual">
    <pose>0 0 0 0 0 3.1415927</pose>
    <geometry><mesh><uri>model://kursad_hook/meshes/locking_arm_locked.stl</uri></mesh></geometry>
    <material>
      <ambient>0.55 0.45 0.05 1</ambient>
      <diffuse>0.85 0.70 0.10 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.5 0.5 0.5 1</specular>
    </material>
  </visual>
  <visual name="hook_locking_arm_3_visual">
    <pose>0 0 0 0 0 4.7123890</pose>
    <geometry><mesh><uri>model://kursad_hook/meshes/locking_arm_locked.stl</uri></mesh></geometry>
    <material>
      <ambient>0.55 0.45 0.05 1</ambient>
      <diffuse>0.85 0.70 0.10 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.5 0.5 0.5 1</specular>
    </material>
  </visual>


  <!--
    MIKNATIS (hook_magnet, O10 x 2 mm). PCBV1_PARAMETERS.txt bunu KANCANIN
    parcasi olarak tanimliyor: hook_magnet_diameter 10.00,
    hook_magnet_thickness 2.00. Kanca indiginde yukun O13.00 miknatis
    yuvasina girer ve celik hedef diskin 0.20 mm ustunde durur.

    SADECE GORSEL: manyetik cekim simule EDILMIYOR. Yakalama kurali
    mesafeye dayali ve gorev tarafinda duruyor
    (gz_payload_actuator.py, HOOK_MAGNET_CAPTURE_RADIUS_M = 0.05) - bu
    disk o kuralin nereye baktigini gozle gorulur kiliyor, degistirmiyor.
    Collision yok, yani kancanin 24 mm'lik tek collider'i degismedi.
  -->
  <visual name="hook_magnet_visual">
    <pose>0 0 -0.06565 0 0 0</pose>
    <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.005</radius><length>0.002</length></cylinder></geometry>
    <material>
      <ambient>0.30 0.30 0.32 1</ambient>
      <diffuse>0.62 0.62 0.66 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.9 0.9 0.9 1</specular>
    </material>
  </visual>

  <!--
    COLLISION: DELIBERATELY STILL A 24 mm PRIMITIVE, NOT THE 39848-TRIANGLE
    CAD MESH, AND NOT THE 45 mm SHELL.

    Two independent reasons, both measured rather than assumed:

    1. The mission depends on this size. gz_payload_actuator.py sizes its
       capture rule as "uc (0.012) + yuva (0.015)" - the 0.012 here IS that
       tip radius. Growing the collider to the real 45 mm shell would change
       the pickup geometry the HOOK_MAGNET_CAPTURE_RADIUS_M = 0.05 rule was
       measured against.

    2. A 45 mm shell collider would INTERPENETRATE both payloads. The
       payloads sit directly under the vehicle at |y| in [0.010, 0.060],
       x in [-0.070, +0.070], base_link z in [-0.240, -0.170]; a 22.5 mm
       radius at x = -0.090 reaches x = -0.0675 and overlaps them by 2.5 mm
       in x over the full 27.65 mm of shared height. The world SDF's own F2
       note records what that costs: DART sees an interpenetrating pair the
       moment a joint releases and EJECTS the payload (measured 4.9 m off
       target). A 12 mm radius reaches only x = -0.078 and clears the
       payloads by 8 mm.

    So the visual is the true CAD and the collider is the interface it
    actually touches: the gripper mouth, placed at the bottom face of the
    assembly rather than 64.65 mm up at the rope end where the old sphere
    was. Name and topic are unchanged so nothing downstream moves.
  -->
  <collision name="hook_tip_collision">
    <pose>0 0 -0.0547 0 0 0</pose>
    <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.012</radius><length>0.020</length></cylinder></geometry>
  </collision>

  <sensor name="hook_contact_sensor" type="contact">
    <always_on>true</always_on>
    <update_rate>50</update_rate>
    <contact>
      <collision>hook_tip_collision</collision>
      <topic>/hook/contact</topic>
    </contact>
  </sensor>
</link>

<!-- Rigid: the hook is bolted to the rope end through the M3x20 cross-bolt
     in Core_Upper, so a fixed joint is the honest model. It also means the
     hook adds no degrees of freedom to a vehicle whose flight dynamics are
     already tuned. -->
<joint name="HookBodyJoint" type="fixed">
  <parent>hook_rope_seg_4</parent>
  <child>hook_body_link</child>
</joint>


<!-- ==================== IP (ROPE) - HINGED CHAIN ==================== -->
<!--
  The rope used to be ONE rigid cylinder rigidly welded between the winch and
  the hook: it could telescope but it could not swing, so the hook tracked the
  airframe exactly and a slung load behaved like a bolted-on mast.

  It is now four segments joined by universal (2-axis) joints, so the load
  behaves like a real pendulum: it lags on acceleration, overshoots on stop,
  and hangs vertical while the airframe pitches. Total length is unchanged at
  0.183 m (4 x 0.04575), and the top of segment 1 is still at the drum centre
  with the hook still arriving at base_link z = -0.133, so nothing downstream
  of the rope moved.

  BENDING STIFFNESS (spring_stiffness 0.004 N m/rad, limits +-0.7 rad) is
  NOT a fudge - this line is not a limp string. The V4 hook carries its servo
  INSIDE the hook body, so the line running to it is a cable bundle, and a
  cable bundle has real bending stiffness.

  It also has to be modelled, because a limp cord silently breaks the mission.
  gz_payload_actuator.py pays out HOOK_WINCH_EXTEND_M = 0.40 m ON PURPOSE and
  relies on "physics stops it": with the old RIGID rope the hook pressed on
  the seat and stalled the joint (its own note records cmd 0.12 stalling at
  0.059). A cord with no bending stiffness cannot transmit that compression -
  it just buckles, the winch runs to its full commanded travel, and the slack
  topples the hook. MEASURED at 0.004: with no spring the grounded hook tipped
  to 49 deg, i.e. the magnet pointed sideways instead of down at the payload's
  well. The spring keeps it upright while the slack bows out sideways, which
  is what a stiff lanyard actually does.

  The spring is weak against gravity by design (0.004 vs the pendulum's
  k = mgL = 0.0198 N m/rad at the top joint), so it stiffens the swing by
  ~20% rather than suppressing it.

  NO COLLISION on any segment - deliberately. The cord is visual and inertial
  only; the sole collider on this chain is the hook's own tip. That keeps the
  rope from generating contacts against the airframe, the payloads or itself,
  which is where a segmented rope normally destabilises a sim.

  DAMPING 0.0015 per axis, TUNED AGAINST A MEASUREMENT, not derived. The
  pendulum is m = 0.011 kg at L = 0.183 m, so k = mgL = 0.0198 N m/rad,
  I = mL^2 = 3.68e-4 and critical damping is 5.4e-3. Hand-splitting that over
  four joints predicted zeta ~ 0.2 at 0.0003/axis; flying a 3 m/s lateral step
  and logging the hook measured zeta ~ 0.03 instead - the prediction was off
  by ~7x because the chain's compliance is shared, not per-joint. 0.0015 is
  the value that actually lands the swing where it belongs.

  The target is NOT "as damped as possible". A real cord settles slowly, so
  over-damping would look wrong; the constraint is the mission's own
  HOOK_SETTLE_S = 3.0 s wait after lock (gz_payload_actuator.py). The tune
  keeps several visible swings while decaying inside that budget.

  Measured period is 0.899 s against an ideal point-mass pendulum's 0.858 s.
  The excess is correct and expected: the cord carries its own distributed
  mass, which lengthens the period. RETUNE HERE if the rope length or the
  hook mass changes - zeta scales with the pendulum, not with this number.
-->
<link name="hook_rope_seg_1">
  <pose relative_to="hook_rope_link">0 0 -0.02287 0 0 0</pose>
  <inertial>
    <mass>0.0025</mass>
    <!--
      MASS AND INERTIA ARE BOTH FLOORS, NOT THE TRUE VALUES of a 4 mm x 46 mm
      cord segment (which would be ~0.75 g and ~1.3e-7). They are set by a
      MEASURED failure, not by taste.

      At 0.00075 kg / 1e-6 the cord was fine carrying the 11 g hook, and a
      pickup that RETRACTED the winch first lifted a payload to 14 m without
      complaint. But the mission locks on and then climbs with the winch still
      PAID OUT ("vinc acik, yuk ipte"), so a 0.15 kg payload hung off 0.75 g
      links through a slack, buckled chain - a 200:1 mass ratio per link. That
      configuration diverged and took the whole server down inside ODE:
          Physics::Update -> dartsim -> ConstraintSolver::updateConstraints
          -> GzOdeCollisionDetector::collide -> dxHashSpace::collide
          -> dDebug -> abort
      i.e. a geometry transform had already gone non-finite by the time
      collision ran.

      0.0025 kg / 1e-5 cuts the per-link mass ratio to 60:1 and the inertia
      ratio by 10x. The cord still weighs 10 g in total against a 20 g hook,
      so it still hangs and swings as a cord rather than a chain of weights.
    -->
    <inertia>
      <ixx>1.0e-5</ixx><iyy>1.0e-5</iyy><izz>1.0e-6</izz>
      <ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyz>0</iyz>
    </inertia>
  </inertial>
  <visual name="rope_seg_1_visual">
    <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.004</radius><length>0.04575</length></cylinder></geometry>
    <material>
      <ambient>0.12 0.12 0.12 1</ambient>
      <diffuse>0.18 0.18 0.18 1</diffuse>
    </material>
  </visual>
</link>

<joint name="HookRopeSwing1" type="universal">
  <parent>hook_rope_link</parent>
  <child>hook_rope_seg_1</child>
  <!-- Joint sits at the TOP face of this segment, i.e. where it hangs from
       the one above. Two perpendicular swing axes = a Hooke joint, which is
       what a cord does; no twist DOF, which a cord barely has and which
       would only add a rattling mode. -->
  <pose>0 0 0.02287 0 0 0</pose>
  <axis>
    <xyz expressed_in="__model__">1 0 0</xyz>
    <limit><lower>-1.2</lower><upper>1.2</upper></limit>
    <dynamics>
      <damping>0.0030</damping>
      <friction>0</friction>
      <spring_stiffness>0.004</spring_stiffness>
      <spring_reference>0</spring_reference>
    </dynamics>
  </axis>
  <axis2>
    <xyz expressed_in="__model__">0 1 0</xyz>
    <limit><lower>-1.2</lower><upper>1.2</upper></limit>
    <dynamics>
      <damping>0.0030</damping>
      <friction>0</friction>
      <spring_stiffness>0.004</spring_stiffness>
      <spring_reference>0</spring_reference>
    </dynamics>
  </axis2>
</joint>

<link name="hook_rope_seg_2">
  <pose relative_to="hook_rope_seg_1">0 0 -0.04575 0 0 0</pose>
  <inertial>
    <mass>0.0025</mass>
    <!--
      MASS AND INERTIA ARE BOTH FLOORS, NOT THE TRUE VALUES of a 4 mm x 46 mm
      cord segment (which would be ~0.75 g and ~1.3e-7). They are set by a
      MEASURED failure, not by taste.

      At 0.00075 kg / 1e-6 the cord was fine carrying the 11 g hook, and a
      pickup that RETRACTED the winch first lifted a payload to 14 m without
      complaint. But the mission locks on and then climbs with the winch still
      PAID OUT ("vinc acik, yuk ipte"), so a 0.15 kg payload hung off 0.75 g
      links through a slack, buckled chain - a 200:1 mass ratio per link. That
      configuration diverged and took the whole server down inside ODE:
          Physics::Update -> dartsim -> ConstraintSolver::updateConstraints
          -> GzOdeCollisionDetector::collide -> dxHashSpace::collide
          -> dDebug -> abort
      i.e. a geometry transform had already gone non-finite by the time
      collision ran.

      0.0025 kg / 1e-5 cuts the per-link mass ratio to 60:1 and the inertia
      ratio by 10x. The cord still weighs 10 g in total against a 20 g hook,
      so it still hangs and swings as a cord rather than a chain of weights.
    -->
    <inertia>
      <ixx>1.0e-5</ixx><iyy>1.0e-5</iyy><izz>1.0e-6</izz>
      <ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyz>0</iyz>
    </inertia>
  </inertial>
  <visual name="rope_seg_2_visual">
    <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.004</radius><length>0.04575</length></cylinder></geometry>
    <material>
      <ambient>0.12 0.12 0.12 1</ambient>
      <diffuse>0.18 0.18 0.18 1</diffuse>
    </material>
  </visual>
</link>

<joint name="HookRopeSwing2" type="universal">
  <parent>hook_rope_seg_1</parent>
  <child>hook_rope_seg_2</child>
  <!-- Joint sits at the TOP face of this segment, i.e. where it hangs from
       the one above. Two perpendicular swing axes = a Hooke joint, which is
       what a cord does; no twist DOF, which a cord barely has and which
       would only add a rattling mode. -->
  <pose>0 0 0.02287 0 0 0</pose>
  <axis>
    <xyz expressed_in="__model__">1 0 0</xyz>
    <limit><lower>-1.2</lower><upper>1.2</upper></limit>
    <dynamics>
      <damping>0.0030</damping>
      <friction>0</friction>
      <spring_stiffness>0.004</spring_stiffness>
      <spring_reference>0</spring_reference>
    </dynamics>
  </axis>
  <axis2>
    <xyz expressed_in="__model__">0 1 0</xyz>
    <limit><lower>-1.2</lower><upper>1.2</upper></limit>
    <dynamics>
      <damping>0.0030</damping>
      <friction>0</friction>
      <spring_stiffness>0.004</spring_stiffness>
      <spring_reference>0</spring_reference>
    </dynamics>
  </axis2>
</joint>

